---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import TokenTable from "../components/api/TokenTable.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "anatomy", label: "Anatomy", heading: false },
  { id: "items", label: "Items" },
  { id: "color", label: "Color" },
  { id: "narrow", label: "Narrow windows" },
  { id: "placement", label: "Placement" },
  { id: "accessibility", label: "Accessibility" },
  { id: "tokens", label: "Tokens" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="status-bar" sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.anatomy}>
        <p class="flow-text">
          A status bar is a thin row of app state along the bottom of an app
          shell, like the one in a code editor. It shows things such as the
          branch, problem counts and the cursor position, and each item can
          open what it describes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Write it as a <code>&lt;footer class="status-bar"&gt;</code>. Its
          direct children are the items. <code>.end</code> on the first
          trailing item pushes that item and the ones after it to the end edge.
          The status bar is CSS only. Material Design has no status bar
          component, and in Material "status bar" means the phone's system bar,
          so this one follows the code editor pattern instead.
        </p>
        <div data-markdown-ignore>
          <footer class="status-bar">
            <button type="button" class="primary on-primary-text" aria-label="Remote: SSH devbox">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">terminal</span>
            </button>
            <button type="button" class="menu-trigger" data-target="status-bar-branch-menu" data-menu-options='{"placement": "top"}' aria-label="Branch master, switch branch">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">fork_right</span>
              <span>master</span>
            </button>
            <a href="#accessibility" aria-label="0 errors, 2 warnings">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">error</span><span>0</span>
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">warning</span><span>2</span>
            </a>
            <span role="status">Ln 42, Col 7</span>
            <span data-priority="low">UTF-8</span>
            <button type="button" class="end" aria-label="Notifications, 3 new">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">notifications<span class="badge">3</span></span>
            </button>
          </footer>
          <menu id="status-bar-branch-menu">
            <li><button type="button">master</button></li>
            <li><button type="button">feature/status-bar</button></li>
          </menu>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<footer class="status-bar">
  <button type="button" class="primary on-primary-text" aria-label="Remote: SSH devbox">
    <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">terminal</span>
  </button>
  <button type="button" class="menu-trigger" data-target="branch-menu" data-menu-options='{"placement": "top"}' aria-label="Branch master, switch branch">
    <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">fork_right</span>
    <span>master</span>
  </button>
  <a href="#problems" aria-label="0 errors, 2 warnings">
    <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">error</span><span>0</span>
    <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">warning</span><span>2</span>
  </a>
  <span role="status">Ln 42, Col 7</span>
  <span data-priority="low">UTF-8</span>
  <button type="button" class="end" aria-label="Notifications, 3 new">
    <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">notifications<span class="badge">3</span></span>
  </button>
</footer>
<menu id="branch-menu">
  <li><button type="button">master</button></li>
  <li><button type="button">feature/status-bar</button></li>
</menu>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.items}>
        <p>Pick the element by what the item does:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> opens something, such as a <a href="menu.html">menu</a> or a dialog. Give a menu trigger <code>{`data-menu-options='{"placement": "top"}'`}</code> so the menu opens above the bar.</li>
          <li>An <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> goes somewhere, such as the problems panel.</li>
          <li>A <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> is read-only text. It has no hover state and is not a tab stop.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          A disabled button or link (<code>disabled</code>,
          <code>.disabled</code> or <code>aria-disabled="true"</code>) is drawn
          at 38% opacity. Button items don't take the common button styles,
          so <code>.tonal</code>, <code>.outlined</code> and toggle colors
          don't apply inside the bar.
        </p>
        <p>
          An item holds an icon, text in a <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>, or both.
          A <a href="badges.html">badge</a> nested in the icon sits on the
          icon's corner and may rise above the bar. Items are 24px targets with
          16px icons and 8px of padding on each side.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.color}>
        <p>
          <code>.vibrant</code> paints the whole bar primary-container, the
          same colors as a vibrant <a href="toolbars.html">toolbar</a>. Use it
          for a mode the user has to notice, such as a debugging session.
        </p>
        <p>
          To color one item, use the <a href="color.html">color helpers</a>,
          such as <code>primary on-primary-text</code> or
          <code>error-container on-error-container-text</code>. Hover still
          shows over the fill. The text has to say what the color means,
          because a screen reader doesn't announce it and forced colors remove
          it.
        </p>
        <div data-markdown-ignore>
          <footer class="status-bar vibrant">
            <span role="status">Debugging: server.js</span>
            <span class="error-container on-error-container-text">Build failed</span>
            <button type="button" class="end">Stop</button>
          </footer>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<footer class="status-bar vibrant">
  <span role="status">Debugging: server.js</span>
  <span class="error-container on-error-container-text">Build failed</span>
  <button type="button" class="end">Stop</button>
</footer>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.narrow}>
        <p>
          Below 600px, items with <code>data-priority="low"</code> are hidden.
          Mark the items people can do without, such as the encoding or the
          indentation size. If that hides the first <code>.end</code> item,
          the next visible <code>.end</code> item starts the end group. Items
          that still don't fit wrap to a second row, so the page never scrolls
          sideways and every item stays reachable.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.placement}>
        <p>
          Put the bar last in the app shell. It sticks to the bottom of a page
          that scrolls and pads for the bottom safe area on phones.
        </p>
        <p>
          A <code>&lt;footer&gt;</code> that is a child of
          <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> is the page's footer landmark. If the page
          already has a site footer, write the bar as
          <code>&lt;div class="status-bar" role="region" aria-label="Status"&gt;</code>
          instead, so the page doesn't end up with two footer landmarks. Keep
          the role: ARIA ignores an <code>aria-label</code> on a plain
          <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Don't show a status bar at the same time as a
          <a href="navigation-bar.html">navigation bar</a>. Both sit at the
          bottom of the window. Its 24px items are too small for a
          touch-first layout, so hide the bar there or move what it shows
          into the page.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.accessibility}>
        <p>
          The bar takes no <code>role="toolbar"</code>. Every item is its own
          tab stop, and arrow keys do nothing. It isn't a live region either.
          Put <code>role="status"</code> on the one text item whose text
          changes, such as the cursor position, never on the bar. A live
          region on the bar would read every button label with each update.
        </p>
        <p>
          A button or link with only an icon needs an
          <code>aria-label</code>. When the label stands in for visible
          numbers, repeat them, as in <code>aria-label="0 errors, 2 warnings"</code>.
          Items meet the WCAG 2.5.8 minimum target size of 24px, but not
          Material's 48dp.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.tokens}>
        <p>Override these on the <code>.status-bar</code>.</p>
        <TokenTable>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-status-bar-container-height</code></td><td>1.5rem (24px)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-status-bar-container-color</code></td><td><code>--md-sys-color-surface-container</code> (<code>primary-container</code> when vibrant)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-status-bar-color</code></td><td><code>--md-sys-color-on-surface-variant</code> (<code>on-primary-container</code> when vibrant)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-status-bar-item-space</code></td><td>8px</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-status-bar-icon-size</code></td><td>1rem (16px)</td></tr>
        </TokenTable>
  </Section>
</DocsLayout>
